Share native members through persistent indexes

Avoid copying complete inherited member relations at every morphism.
Keep monotone mappings as compressed offset runs through prefix keys
and suffix values, compose compatible transforms, and preserve shared
subtrees during transport, comparisons, and incremental index updates.
Retain eager paths for small relations and non-injective images.

For declared acyclic total inheritance, share materialized owner-local
indexes in stable origin coordinates. Persistent member domains and
partitions retain local additions and equations without allocating
individual inherited images. Virtual handle blocks preserve distinct
public identities for each owner. Rebase from parent snapshots and
replay only local support and quotient changes.

Index equality work by the losing representative's incidence bucket.
Unary relations already provide that index through their row set.
Preserve unchanged relation roots so publication and consumer histories
can skip unrelated updates. Extend secondary indexes with new owned
rows instead of repeatedly unioning their entire previous seed.

Reuse inherited closure through ordinary rule input histories. Require
exact processed source snapshots and completed transport, and retain
normal matching while existential definitions are pending. Compare
current rows with the unmodified inherited roots so target equality
makes rewritten rows new matching work. When parent facts advance,
replay local rows to preserve joins with facts processed earlier; then
resume ordinary temporal deltas. Track member revisions as well as
relation revisions so pure domain growth follows the same contract.

Use the same mechanism for predicates and functionality. Keep
owner-dependent global premises and owner-valued data on the ordinary
path when their matches do not transport. Prefer selective owner-bound
joins while preserving premise ages, canonical binding checks, and
private noncanonical witnesses.

Keep public lookups, iteration, equality, and handles consistent across
compact and ordinary storage. Materialize unsupported operations before
mutation, retaining pending outputs, allocated elements, and live total
inheritance policies. Check cumulative owner and morphism quotients
before accepting endpoints that could introduce cycles. Parallel
morphisms keep independent images. Dynamic exports remain snapshots of
current state and do not serialize live total inheritance policies.

Keep compact activation conservative for nested, cross-owner, and
unsupported relation layouts. Ordinary partial mappings retain their
existing path. Preserve historical snapshots and finite evaluation
fuel through source growth, equality, and storage transitions.

Reduce ordinary tree overhead with compact leaves, persistent interval
sets, path-only updates, shared cursors, and stack-safe destruction.
The generic WBTreeMap lazy caches and destruction changes tighten some
borrowed-value lifetime behavior: previous covariance and some early
dropping examples are not preserved. Generated evaluators own values.

Regression coverage executes native and desugared evaluators with both
evaluation strategies. It includes partial/conflicting images, private
aliases, local and inherited functionality conflicts, late growth,
owner/morphism equality, fallback, pending definitions, and randomized
persistent-tree mutations. Standalone COW comparisons and their plots
are temporary artifacts rather than repository benchmarks.

Keep compact regression theories under the ordinary Cargo fixture
directory so the generated evaluators run in the full test suite.

Exercise inherited closure through public evaluator operations,
including recursive local/inherited joins, target equality, late parent
changes, owner-valued data, and pending existential consequences across
partial closure. Keep functionality coverage independent of internal
validation hooks by checking public lookups and equality.
